
## 1. Input/Output Systems## Detailed Architectural Breakdown
To truly understand Java I/O, you must look at how the JVM bridges the gap between your application code and the operating system's kernel.

* Scanner CPU Overhead: When you call scanner.nextInt(), the framework does not just read bytes; it scans the stream using a heavily configured java.util.regex.Pattern. This requires state-machine evaluation to find delimiters, strip whitespace, and parse the characters into a binary primitive. This creates massive CPU overhead in high-throughput data processing pipelines.
* BufferedReader and Stream Chaining: BufferedReader is decoupled from the underlying data source. It uses the Decorator Design Pattern. You chain a raw byte stream (InputStream), a character decoder (InputStreamReader), and a buffered processor (BufferedReader) together.

+------------------+     Bytes     +---------------------+    Chars    +----------------+

|    System.in     | ------------> |  InputStreamReader  | ----------> | BufferedReader |
| (Raw Byte Stream)|               | (Decoder/Bridge)    |             | (8KB Buffer)   |
+------------------+               +---------------------+             +----------------+
                                                                               |
                                                                               v
                                                                        [JVM Thread]


* OS System Calls: Every time an application requests data from disk or network without a buffer, the JVM must trigger an explicit context switch from User Mode to Kernel Mode via a read() system call. Kernel context switches are expensive because they require saving CPU registers and flushing caches. BufferedReader minimizes this by pulling an entire 8 KB block into memory in a single system call.

## 5-Year Experience Interview Edge

* The Scanner Vulnerability: If a candidate suggests using Scanner for processing large-scale enterprise log files, it is an immediate red flag. A 5-year developer should know that Scanner is not thread-safe and its synchronized state engine will bottleneck concurrent operations.
* The java.nio Alternative: In modern, highly concurrent systems, even BufferedReader (which uses blocking I/O) can be suboptimal. Be prepared to discuss Java NIO (Non-blocking I/O), specifically Files.newBufferedReader() or using FileChannel with ByteBuffer for zero-copy memory mapping (MappedByteBuffer), which bypasses JVM memory spaces entirely by reading data directly into native memory.

