## 2. Primitive Data Types & Memory Allocation## Detailed Architectural Breakdown
Memory efficiency and predictable execution depend entirely on where data lives and how it is padded.

* Boolean Array Byte Layout: While a standalone boolean uses a 4-byte int slot on the stack for architectural alignment efficiency (32-bit slot processing), a boolean[] array is packed by the JVM into a continuous byte array where each element takes exactly 1 byte (8 bits).
* Object Header Layout: Every wrapper object on the Heap carries a tax known as the object header:
* Mark Word (8 bytes): Stores identity hashcode, GC generation age, locking states (biased, thin, fat), and biased threading info.
   * Klass Word (4-8 bytes): A pointer to the native metadata instance of the class type. (Often 4 bytes due to Compressed OOPs—Ordinary Object Pointers).
   * Padding: Objects are aligned to 8-byte boundaries. An Integer wrapper containing a 4-byte primitive actually consumes 16 bytes of heap memory, creating a 400% memory footprint tax compared to a raw int.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

|                           Integer Object Layout                       |
+-------------------+-------------------+---------------+---------------+

|     Mark Word     |    Klass Word     | Primitive int |    Padding    |
|     (8 Bytes)     |     (4 Bytes)     |   (4 Bytes)   |   (0 Bytes)   |
+-------------------+-------------------+---------------+---------------+

| <-------------------------- Total: 16 Bytes ------------------------> |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+


* Integer Cache Subtleties: Integer.valueOf(int) reads from an internal static cache (IntegerCache). While the lower bound is hardcoded at -128, the upper bound (127) can be tuned at JVM startup using the -XX:AutoBoxCacheMax=<size> flag.
* Two's Complement & Silent Wraparound: Java doesn't throw an arithmetic exception on integer overflow. At the bitwise level, adding 1 to 0x7FFFFFFF (Integer.MAX_VALUE: 01111111 11111111 11111111 11111111) turns the sign bit to 1 (10000000 00000000 00000000 00000000), which represents Integer.MIN_VALUE (-2147483648).

## 5-Year Experience Interview Edge

* Autoboxing Performance Pitfalls: Interviews frequently test your awareness of boxing in loops. For instance, declaring Long sum = 0L; instead of long sum = 0L; inside a loop that runs millions of times will create millions of temporary Long objects. This triggers high memory usage and frequent Garbage Collection (GC) pauses.
* Safe Arithmetic: Show that you know how to handle arithmetic safely. Mention Math.addExact(a, b) or Math.multiplyExact(a, b). These methods throw an ArithmeticException if an overflow occurs, keeping your data accurate in financial settings.

