## 3. Language Operators, Flow Control & Boolean Logic## Detailed Architectural Breakdown
The JVM uses distinct optimizations for controlling logic flows and compilation strategies.

* Short-Circuit vs. Bitwise Guarding:
* if (obj != null && obj.isValid()) executes safely because && acts as a guard clause.
   * if (obj != null & obj.isValid()) evaluates both sides. If obj is null, this will throw a NullPointerException because the right side runs anyway.
* Bytecode Compilation of Switch: The compiler optimizes switch statements into two distinct bytecode instructions depending on how close the values are to each other:
* tableswitch: Used when the cases have sequential or near-sequential integer keys (e.g., 1, 2, 3). The JVM creates a direct jump table indexed by the key. This allows for constant-time O(1) performance.
   * lookupswitch: Used when case keys are sparse or widespread (e.g., 100, 5000, 900000). The values are sorted in the bytecode, and the JVM performs a binary search ($O(\log N)$ complexity) to locate the correct branch.

## 5-Year Experience Interview Edge

* Switch Pattern Matching (Java 21+): You should know about recent updates to pattern matching in switches. This feature lets you match based on object type and use clean guard conditions directly inside your case branches.
* Null Handling in Switch: In older versions of Java, passing a null variable to a switch threw an immediate NullPointerException. Since Java 21, you can explicitly handle this inside the switch block by defining a case null -> branch.

// Modern Java 21+ Pattern Matching Switchpublic String formatInput(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case null -> "Target is completely null";
        case Integer i -> String.format("Integer value: %d", i);
        case String s && s.isEmpty() -> "Empty string detected";
        case String s -> String.format("String value: %s", s);
        default -> "Unknown type description";
    };
}

