## 4. Loop Control Structures## Detailed Architectural Breakdown
Loops behave differently under the hood depending on whether you are iterating over raw memory or an object collection.

* Bytecode Erasure of Enhanced For Loop:
* When applied to a standard array (int[]), the compiler turns the enhanced loop into a fast, index-based for loop, which benefits from CPU cache lines.
   * When applied to any object implementing Iterable<T> (like ArrayList), the compiler rewrites the loop to use an explicit Iterator.

// What you write:for (String item : myList) { System.out.println(item); }
// What the compiler creates:
Iterator<String> it = myList.iterator();while (it.hasNext()) {
    String item = it.next();
    System.out.println(item);
}


* Fail-Fast Mechanics: Collection classes maintain an internal volatile counter called modCount that tracks structural modifications (adds, removes, or resizes). When an iterator is created, it takes a snapshot of this value (expectedModCount = modCount). Every time you call iterator.next(), it verifies that modCount == expectedModCount. If a different thread or your own loop modifies the collection structure directly, a ConcurrentModificationException is thrown instantly.

## 5-Year Experience Interview Edge

* Safe Modifications During Iteration: If an interviewer asks how to safely delete elements from a collection during a loop, explain that you must call iterator.remove() explicitly, use collection.removeIf(predicate), or switch to a thread-safe alternative like CopyOnWriteArrayList.
* Labeled Breaks in Complex Logic: Labeled breaks are sometimes criticized as looking like "goto" statements, but they are useful for escaping nested validation blocks cleanly. They let you exit multiple loops without needing to maintain flags or return early from a method.

