## 5. 1D Array Memory Mechanics## Detailed Architectural Breakdown
Arrays provide low-level memory efficiency but carry safety overhead in the JVM.

* Spatial Locality and Cache Lines: Modern CPUs read data from main memory into L1/L2 caches using fixed blocks called Cache Lines (typically 64 bytes wide). Because elements in a 1D Java array sit right next to each other in memory, loading index 0 often brings the next several elements into the CPU cache automatically. This makes sequential array loops fast.
* Array Allocation on Heap: Unlike languages like C++, Java arrays are always full objects created on the heap, even if they hold primitive types. This means an int[] array requires its own object header alongside memory for the actual integers.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

|                         int[] Array Layout                            |
+-------------------+-------------------+---------------+---------------+

|     Mark Word     |    Klass Word     | Array Length  |  int [0...N]  |
|     (8 Bytes)     |     (4 Bytes)     |   (4 Bytes)   |  (Contiguous) |
+-------------------+-------------------+---------------+---------------+


* Bytecode Execution of Bounds Checks: For every array read or write operation, the JVM issues an explicit arraylength evaluation alongside a comparison operation. If your code tries to access an invalid index, the JVM stops the operation and throws an ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException. This runtime boundary check prevents memory corruption vulnerabilities like buffer overflows.

## 5-Year Experience Interview Edge

* Matrix Traversal Traps (Row-Major Order): If you need to walk through a multi-dimensional array, always process elements row by row (array[row][col]) rather than column by column (array[col][row]). Iterating row-by-row works with Java's memory layout, which minimizes cache misses and boosts loop performance.
* Arrays vs. ArrayList: Understand that ArrayList uses a 1D primitive array internally. When it reaches capacity, it creates a new array that is 1.5 times larger and shifts data over using System.arraycopy(), which performs a direct memory block copy.

