## 6. String Immutability, Memory Pool & Utilities## Detailed Architectural Breakdown
String management is one of the most optimized parts of the Java Runtime Environment.

* Compact Strings Architecture (Java 9+): Older versions of Java saved characters using a 2-byte char[] array in UTF-16 format, which wasted memory for basic Latin text. Modern Java uses a 1-byte byte[] array combined with an internal encoding flag field called coder. If a string contains only Latin-1 characters, the coder is set to 0 and each character takes 1 byte. If it contains complex characters (like emojis or kanji), the coder switches to 1 and uses 2 bytes per character, cutting string memory footprints by up to 50% in web applications.
* String Constant Pool (SCP) Internals: The SCP is a native hashtable (StringTable) located in the JVM's Heap memory. Its bucket count can be configured using -XX:StringTableSize.
* Using String s = "data"; automatically looks up the value in this table.
   * Using String s = new String("data"); forces the JVM to create a completely new object instance on the heap, bypassing the pool optimization unless you explicitly call s.intern().

                  +-----------------------------------+

                  |            JVM Heap               |
                  |                                   |
                  |   +---------------------------+   |
"literal" ----------> | String Constant Pool (SCP)|   |

                  |   |   ["literal"]             |   |
                  |   +---------------------------+   |
                  |                 ^                 |
                  |                 | (Points to)     |
new String() -------> [String Object]                 |

                  |                                   |
                  +-----------------------------------+


* StringBuilder Resizing Mechanics: StringBuilder manages a dynamic byte[] array buffer. When you call .append(), it checks if the extra characters fit. If they do not, it grows the internal array size using a formula of (current_capacity * 2) + 2. It then copies the old data over, so appending elements in a loop can trigger unexpected array allocations if you don't guess the initial size well.

## 5-Year Experience Interview Edge

* Security Implications of Immutability: If an interviewer asks why sensitive information like passwords should be stored in a char[] array rather than a String, you should provide a clear architectural answer: strings stay in the String Constant Pool until the garbage collector clears them out. Because they are immutable, you cannot wipe their contents from memory. A raw char[] array can be overwritten with zeros immediately after use, which protects sensitive data from unexpected memory dumps.
* Compile-Time Optimization (Inlining): Note that simple string concatenations like String s = "a" + "b" + "c"; are optimized during compilation into a single "abc" literal string. You do not need to manually change every single inline string combination to a StringBuilder.

