## 3. Language Operators, Flow Control & Boolean Logic## 5-Year Developer Deep Dive & Interview Mechanics## Short-Circuit Evaluation vs. Bitwise Operations

* && and || (Logical/Lazy Operators): These introduce conditional branch instructions (ifeq, ifne) at the bytecode level. In if (conditionA() && conditionB()), if conditionA() returns false, the JVM execution pointer jumps entirely past the evaluation of conditionB().
* & and | (Bitwise/Eager Operators): When applied to booleans, these do not generate conditional jump instructions. Both methods are executed unconditionally. The JVM loads both values onto the operand stack and applies a bitwise logic instruction (iand, ior).
* Interview Trap: Using logical short-circuiting when the second operand contains a crucial side-effect (e.g., if (isAdmin || updateAuditLog())). If isAdmin is true, the audit log is silently skipped. Conversely, using bitwise operators on non-short-circuiting logic can cause silent NullPointerExceptions: if (obj != null & obj.isValid()) will crash because obj.isValid() executes even if obj is null.

## Strict Boolean Guarding
Unlike languages that compile to unmanaged machine code or use weak typing (C++, JavaScript), Java’s compiler enforces type-safety strictly at compile time. Integers cannot be implicitly cast to booleans. At the bytecode level, condition control flow relies strictly on operations yielding type Z (Boolean). This guarantees that your compiled branches can never suffer from type-coercion memory bugs or undefined behavior.
## Switch Expressions (Java 14+) & Bytecode Jump Tables
Modern Java switch expressions are not just syntactic sugar; they completely change how control flow is evaluated.

* tableswitch: If your switch constants are tightly packed (e.g., cases 1, 2, 3, 4), the compiler generates a tableswitch instruction. It builds an array-based jump table in memory. Finding the target code block is a direct array index calculation: $O(1)$ time complexity.
* lookupswitch: If your switch constants are sparse or jagged (e.g., cases 10, 500, 20000), the compiler cannot build a contiguous array without wasting massive memory. It generates a lookupswitch instruction, which pairs sorted keys with branch targets. The JVM performs a binary search to find the match: $O(\log N)$ time complexity.
* String Switches: Switching on a String executes a two-step process: the JVM first switches on the String.hashCode() (using a jump table). Because of potential hash collisions, it then verifies the match using .equals() inside the matched branch.

