## 4. Loop Control Structures## 5-Year Developer Deep Dive & Interview Mechanics## for vs. Enhanced for-each Bytecode Lowering
How a loop performs depends entirely on the data structure it traverses:

* Primitive Arrays: For int[] arr, an enhanced for-each loop is perfectly optimized. The compiler lowers it to a standard index-based for loop under the hood. It utilizes a single arraylength bytecode instruction and clean array bounds checks. It has zero heap allocation overhead.
* Iterable<T> Collections: For a List<T> or Set<T>, an enhanced for-each loop compiles down to an explicit Iterator<T> pattern.

// What you write:for (String item : list) { ... }
// What the compiler creates:
Iterator<String> it = list.iterator();while (it.hasNext()) {
    String item = it.next();
    ...
}


* The Cost: Calling .iterator() instantiates a new iterator object on the Heap. Doing this inside a high-frequency loop or a real-time system poll introduces continuous heap allocation pressure and eventual GC cycles.

## Fail-Fast Mechanics and Multi-Threaded Hazards
The infamous ConcurrentModificationException is managed via an explicit state-tracking structural mechanism.

* Every standard collection maintains a raw protected transient int modCount = 0; field. Every time you call .add(), .remove(), or .clear(), modCount increments.
* When an iterator is initialized, it copies this value into its own private int expectedModCount = modCount;.
* On every single iteration step (.next()), the iterator asserts: if (modCount != expectedModCount) throw new ConcurrentModificationException();.
* Interview Tip: If you must modify a collection while iterating over it, never use the collection's direct modification methods. Use iterator.remove(), which updates expectedModCount internally to stay in sync, or transition to a concurrent collection like CopyOnWriteArrayList if multi-threaded modifications are occurring.

## Labeled Breaks (break labelName;)
At the bytecode level, labeled jumps map directly to unconditional jump instructions (goto). Unlike traditional multi-level nested flag checks (boolean found = false; if(found) break;), a labeled break unwinds the operand stack frame immediately and resets the JVM instruction pointer directly to the target block. This is highly efficient and clean for complex parsing algorithms.
