## 6. String Immutability, Memory Pool & Utilities## 5-Year Developer Deep Dive & Interview Mechanics## Immutability Architecture & Compact Strings
Prior to Java 9, strings were backed by a char[] array (taking 2 bytes per character). Modern Java uses Compact Strings:

* Backed by a dense private final byte[] value; array along with a single 1-byte coder flag.
* If a string contains only Latin-1 characters, the coder is set to 0 and it packs characters into 1 byte each, instantly slashing the heap consumption of Strings across your enterprise applications by 50%.
* Why final?: Immutability ensures absolute thread safety. Multiple threads can read the same string instance without synchronization lock overhead. It also allows the string to cache its own hash code safely (private int hash;). Since the characters cannot change, the hash is computed lazily exactly once. If it's zero, it calculates it; otherwise, it returns the cached integer instantly. This makes strings incredibly fast when used as keys in a HashMap.

## String Constant Pool (SCP) Mechanics
The SCP is a native StringTable implemented as a fixed-capacity hashtable located inside the JVM Heap (specifically within the Old/Tenured Generation in modern JVMs).

* Compile-Time Optimization: String literals like String s = "Java"; do not create run-time instances if they already exist. The compiler puts them into the class file's constant pool, and they are linked to the SCP during class resolution.
* str.intern(): Calling .intern() instructs the JVM to check if an equal string exists in the StringTable.
* If it exists, it discards the runtime heap object and returns the cached pool reference.
   * If it doesn't, it moves that string instance into the pool.
* Production Warning: Be incredibly careful with .intern(). If you blindly intern millions of unique runtime strings (like raw user tracking IDs), you will bloat the native StringTable. This leads to hash collisions within the pool itself, turning an $O(1)$ reference lookup into a slow linear $O(N)$ scan, significantly degrading JVM bootstrap and performance.

## StringBuilder vs. StringBuffer & Loop Pitfalls

* StringBuilder: A non-synchronized, mutable wrapper around a resizable byte[] array. It is highly efficient for single-threaded modifications.
* StringBuffer: An ancient thread-safe variant where every single method (append, insert) is explicitly marked synchronized. It carries heavy thread-locking overhead and should be avoided in favor of local thread variables or external locking.
* The Concatenation Loop Nightmare:

// Bad practice:
String text = "";for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
    text += i; 
}

At compilation time, the Java compiler converts text += i into: text = new StringBuilder().append(text).append(i).toString();.
This loop instantiates 1000 independent StringBuilder objects and executes 1000 deep array copy operations (System.arraycopy) on each pass. This is a classic anti-pattern that creates instant garbage collection pauses in mid-level code. Always declare a single explicit StringBuilder before entering your loop blocks.

