## What to Avoid: The Hidden Traps of Autoboxing
As a 5-year experienced developer, you must recognize that autoboxing looks clean but introduces structural performance issues. In high-throughput or low-latency systems, these mistakes can degrade application performance, bloat the heap, and cause unpredictable latency spikes.
## 1. Avoid Primitive Accumulators with Wrapper Objects [1] 
When you use a wrapper class (Long, Integer, Double) as an accumulator variable inside a loop, every single mathematical operations creates a new object.

* The Code to Avoid:

Long sum = 0L; // Wrapper Objectfor (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    sum += i; // Implicitly becomes: sum = Long.valueOf(sum.longValue() + i);
}

* Why it breaks: Wrapper objects are immutable. The JVM cannot modify the value inside the existing Long object. Instead, it must unbox the current value, perform the addition, and box the result into a brand-new Long object on the heap. Running this one million times creates 1,000,000 short-lived objects that instantly die, clogging the Young Generation space of the heap. [2, 3, 4, 5] 

## 2. Avoid Using Wrapper Classes in Performance-Critical Collection Loops [6, 7] 
Iterating over a collection of wrapper objects to calculate aggregates forces unboxing on every single iteration step.

* The Code to Avoid:

List<Integer> scores = getMilionScores();int total = 0;for (Integer score : scores) { // Forces unboxing on every loop step
    if (score != null) {       // Forces manual null checks
        total += score; 
    }
}

* Why it breaks: You pay a double penalty. First, you suffer from CPU cache misses because List<Integer> stores references scattered across the heap rather than a contiguous block of data. Second, the CPU wastes cycles constantly calling .intValue() to do basic math.

## 3. Avoid Blindly Trusting Flyweight Caches (valueOf)
Java caches integers from -128 to 127. [8] 

* The Trap: If your loop processes metrics, IDs, or counters that occasionally cross 127, your code will suddenly shift gears. Below 127, it reuses cached instances (fast, low memory). Above 127, it silently begins allocating memory on the heap. This makes performance profiling highly inconsistent. [9] 

