## The Operational Impact: Why Interactivity Experts Care
If you explain why this matters mechanically during an interview, you demonstrate senior-level architectural awareness.

[Loop Iteration] 
   └── 1. Unbox Object  --> Fetch primitive value from Heap to Stack
   └── 2. Math Operation --> CPU adds values
   └── 3. Box Result    --> Allocate brand new Object on Heap
   └── 4. Heap Bloat    --> Young Gen fills up rapidly
   └── 5. GC Triggered  --> Minor/Major GC halts application threads (STW)


   1. Garbage Collection (GC) Thrashing: Allocating millions of temporary objects triggers frequent Minor GCs in the Young Generation (specifically the Eden space). While modern GCs like G1 or ZGC handle short-lived objects well, high allocation rates force frequent collections, consuming CPU cycles that should be spent on business logic. [10, 11, 12, 13, 14] 
   2. Stop-The-World (STW) Pauses: If the allocation rate outpaces the GC cleaning rate, the JVM will stall your application threads entirely (STW) to catch up on cleanup, destroying your API's SLA response-time metrics. [15, 16] 
   3. Memory Footprint Bloat: Because an object header takes 12–16 bytes plus padding, storing millions of boxed primitives uses up to 4x more RAM than storing raw primitives.

