## What to Use Instead: High-Performance Alternatives
To completely bypass these overheads, implement the following patterns depending on your specific use case.
## 1. Use Pure Primitive Types for Local Computations [17] 
Always default to primitives (long, int, double) for local variables, counters, loops, and accumulation logic. [18] 

* The Correct Code:

long sum = 0L; // Pure primitive on the Stackfor (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
    sum += i; // In-place modification, zero allocations, blazingly fast
}

* Senior Insight: Local primitives are allocated directly on the Thread Stack. They require zero heap tracking, zero object headers, and are instantly wiped out when the method frame finishes executing, completely bypassing the Garbage Collector. [19] 

## 2. Use Primitive Streams for Functional Aggregations
If you love functional programming styles, never use a generic stream like Stream<Integer>. Use the dedicated, primitive-specialized stream classes: IntStream, LongStream, or DoubleStream. [20, 21] 

* The Correct Code:

// Zero object overhead, highly optimized by the JIT compilerlong totalScore = IntStream.range(0, 1_000_000)
                           .sum(); 

* Why it works: Specialized primitive streams process unboxed raw data directly. They use primitive-based primitives/consumers underneath (like IntConsumer), preventing any boxing or unboxing inside the pipeline operations. [22] 

## 3. Use Contiguous Primitive Arrays
When dealing with large volumes of data, store them in raw primitive arrays (int[], long[]) instead of Collections (ArrayList<Integer>). [23] 

* The Correct Code:

int[] rawData = new int[1_000_000]; // Tailored primitive array// Populate...long sum = 0;for (int value : rawData) {
    sum += value; // Maximizes CPU cache locality (L1/L2 caches)
}

* Why it works: A primitive array stores data sequentially in memory. When the CPU reads the first index, it pre-fetches the next chunk of integers into the L1/L2 hardware cache line (64 bytes). This yields massive performance gains over a List<Integer>, which forces the CPU to constantly fetch random pointer addresses across the RAM. [24, 25] 

## 4. Leverage Specialized Primitive Collections (For High-Throughput Engine Architectures)
If your architecture requires map/set structures but cannot tolerate object wrapper overheads, point out that you skip standard Java Collections entirely in favor of third-party primitive collections like Trove, FastUtil, or Eclipse Collections.

* Example Structure:

